The Sant Bart University bombing was a terrorist attack carried out by the [[Primera Ordre de Balboa]] (POB), a white nationalist group, on 12 February 1975 at the [[Universitat de Sant Bart]] in [[Sant Bart]], [[Jala Province]]. A car bomb detonated outside the Faculty of Law building during morning lectures killed 42 people — mostly Black and mixed students and faculty — and injured 78 others. It was the deadliest terrorist attack in Balboan history and the first major escalation of white nationalist violence following the [[Siege of Murado Ranch]] in March 1974.

The bombing was intended as retaliation for the Alvarado administration's enforcement of the [[Twelve Guarantees]] land redistribution programme and as a warning against the further integration of the interior provinces. The attack shocked the country and produced a wave of national solidarity with the victims, but it also demonstrated the capacity of white nationalist groups to operate with impunity in the hinterland. The bombing directly triggered the [[La Guàrdia Campaign]] (1975–1976), the largest domestic military operation in Balboan history.

== Background ==

{{main|Primera Ordre de Balboa|Siege of Murado Ranch|Twelve Guarantees}}

The [[Primera Ordre de Balboa]] (First Order of Balboa) was a white nationalist organisation that emerged in the interior provinces in the early 1970s, in response to the implementation of the [[Twelve Guarantees]] and the dismantling of the planter oligarchy's political and economic power. The group drew its membership from the families and networks of the white planter class — the same social base that had dominated the [[Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)|rump state]] since 1899 — and from remnants of the constabulary and the planter militias that had operated during the rump state period.

The POB's ideology was rooted in the racial hierarchy of the rump state. The group regarded the Twelve Guarantees as an unconstitutional infringement on Balboan sovereignty, the land redistribution programme as the dispossession of the planter class, and the integration of the interior provinces as a threat to the white Balboan community's political and social position. The group's immediate provocation was the [[Siege of Murado Ranch]] in March 1974, in which the Alvarado administration deployed the military to enforce the expropriation of the [[Murado Ranch]], a large plantation owned by the Murado family, one of the oldest planter dynasties in [[Tramuntana Province]]. The siege ended without bloodshed, but it was perceived by the white nationalist community as an act of federal aggression.

=== The Universitat de Sant Bart ===

The [[Universitat de Sant Bart]] was the principal institution of higher education in the Balboan interior. Founded in 1924 during the rump state period as a colonial-style institution serving the white elite, the university had been opened to Black and mixed-race students under the Twelve Guarantees in the early 1960s. By 1975, the university's student body was predominantly Black and mixed — the result of the Guarantees' anti-discrimination provisions and the BPP government's investment in higher education for the interior provinces. The Faculty of Law, the largest faculty at the university, had become a particular symbol of the post-Guarantees order: it trained the lawyers and civil servants who would administer the reunified state, and its student body was drawn disproportionately from the Black and mixed communities that the Guarantees had enfranchised.

The university's transformation made it a target. For the POB, the Universitat de Sant Bart represented the integration that the group was fighting to reverse: a historically white institution now dominated by the Black and mixed students who were being trained to staff the institutions of the post-reunification state. The Faculty of Law, which produced the lawyers who would enforce the land redistribution and the anti-discrimination provisions, was the most visible symbol of that transformation.

== The bombing ==

The attack was carried out by a cell of three POB operatives. The bomb, an improvised explosive device constructed from ammonium nitrate fertiliser and fuel oil (ANFO), was concealed in a stolen vehicle parked outside the Faculty of Law building on the morning of 12 February 1975. The device was detonated at approximately 09:15, during morning lectures, when the building was at full capacity.

The explosion destroyed the front of the Faculty of Law building and caused structural damage to adjacent buildings. The immediate death toll was 38; four more victims died of their injuries in the following days, bringing the total to 42. The victims were predominantly Black and mixed students and faculty members, ranging in age from 18 to 54. Seventy-eight people were injured, many severely, with burns, shrapnel wounds, and injuries from the building's collapse.

The bombing was the deadliest terrorist attack in Balboan history. The scale of the casualties — produced by the timing of the detonation and the concentration of people in the building — exceeded anything the POB had previously attempted and demonstrated that the group had access to explosives expertise and operational planning capabilities.

== Aftermath ==

The bombing produced an immediate national reaction. President [[Miquel Torrent i Alvarado]] addressed the nation within hours of the attack, declaring it "an act of war against the Guarantees and against the people of Balboa." The BPP leadership called for national unity, and the party's base in Sant Cristòfor's Black and mixed working class organised demonstrations in support of the victims and in support of the government's enforcement of the Guarantees. The [[Adonian Community]] issued a statement condemning the attack and affirming its support for the Guarantees.

The attack also produced a wave of solidarity across racial lines. In Sant Bart, where the white political community had been ambivalent about the Guarantees, the scale of the casualties and the targeting of students produced a shift in public sentiment. White community leaders who had been critical of the land redistribution programme publicly condemned the bombing, and the city's commercial and professional organisations issued statements of support for the university community.

=== Investigation ===

The investigation was conducted by the Balboan constabulary with assistance from the [[International Cooperative Police Force]] (ICPF), the AC body established under the Guarantees to oversee compliance. The investigation identified the three POB operatives within weeks, based on witness accounts, vehicle registration records, and the tracing of the fertiliser used in the bomb. The operatives — [[Joan Ballester i Font]], [[Rafel Soler i Mas]], and [[Pere Vidal i Renom]] — were arrested in a series of raids in Sant Bart and Tramuntana in March and April 1975. All three were convicted of murder and terrorism in 1976 and sentenced to life imprisonment.

The investigation also identified the broader POB network that had supported the attack, including the cell that had provided the explosives expertise and the logistics for the operation. Several members of the network were linked to the Murado militia that had participated in the siege of Murado Ranch, confirming the connection between the land redistribution enforcement and the escalation of white nationalist violence.

=== La Guàrdia Campaign ===

{{main|La Guàrdia Campaign}}

The bombing directly triggered the [[La Guàrdia Campaign]] (1975–1976), a series of military and police incursions into the highlands of [[Jala Province|Jala]] to root out POB training camps and safe havens. The campaign, named after the [[La Guàrdia (comarca)|La Guàrdia comarca]] where the POB had established its primary bases, was the largest domestic military operation in Balboan history and was hailed by the BPP as a necessary defence of the Guarantees. The campaign significantly degraded the POB's operational capacity but also produced controversy: the government granted special arms licenses to [[Biru people|Biru]] communities in the highlands to defend themselves against white nationalist cells, a programme that would have unintended consequences including the 1977 [[Aeroístmus hijacking]].

== Legacy ==

The Sant Bart University bombing remains the deadliest terrorist attack in Balboan history. The Universitat de Sant Bart rebuilt the Faculty of Law building, which was renamed the [[Edifici 42]] (Building 42) in memory of the victims. A memorial on the campus, inscribed with the names of the 42 victims, was dedicated on the first anniversary of the bombing in 1976 and has been the site of annual commemoration ceremonies since.

The bombing is commemorated annually in Balboa as a day of remembrance for the victims of white nationalist terrorism. The anniversary, 12 February, is observed by the BPP and its successor organisations, by the Universitat de Sant Bart, and by the national government. The commemoration has at times been a source of political tension: the BPP has used the anniversary to affirm its commitment to the Guarantees, while the Socialist Front of Balboa (FSB) and other left-wing organisations have used it to criticise the BPP's subsequent economic liberalisation, arguing that the party abandoned the social justice agenda that the bombing was intended to reverse.

